From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tcg/wasm32: the interpreter calls helpers without libffi

libffi's wasm32 port makes every helper call of QEMU-Wasm's interpreter a
call into JavaScript (ffi_call_js): it builds an argument array and a
two-element array for each argument and for the return type, makes a
BigInt of every 64-bit value, calls three exported functions that Asyncify
wraps and calls the helper with Function.prototype.apply.  In WebKit that
garbage stays in the vCPU worker's heap until gigabytes have accumulated:
JavaScriptCore counts the shared WebAssembly memory as live in every
worker's heap (once as the memory's extra memory, once as its buffer), and
a worker collects only after allocating about as much again.

TCG passes helpers only i32 (and pointers) and i64 values and gets back
nothing, an i32 or an i64, so the interpreter now calls a helper through a
pointer of the helper's own WebAssembly type, picked by a generated switch
over the 765 signatures of up to seven arguments (tcg/wasm32-tci-calls.h,
from Porty's build/gen-tci-calls.py, checked against libffi by Porty's
tests/tci-calls-test.py): no JavaScript and no garbage on that path.  A
signature without a case (an Int128) still goes through libffi, which is
said once on stderr; porty_tci_stats() counts both kinds of calls.

ffi_call_js saves the C stack pointer when it is entered and restores it
after the helper returns.  When the helper switched coroutines, Asyncify
enters it again on the rewind with a deeper stack pointer, which it then
keeps; after a call through libffi the stack pointer is now put back.
